vine 1.2.0
prefix: publish
title: Grimoire GitHub publication readiness
---
[publish] Prepare Grimoire for GitHub publication (notstarted)
Files: README.md, LICENSE, Cargo.toml, crates/grimoire/Cargo.toml, .github/workflows/ci.yml, examples/reference.grimoire, documentation, the static viewer, and repository metadata.
Do: Prepare the current Grimoire v1 static artifact for a public GitHub repository without expanding its product boundary. Keep the core specification, concrete grammar, static Rust implementation, reference fixtures, workflow guidance, and offline viewer mutually consistent. Treat runtime execution, continuous estimators, parameter updates, causal interventions, fixed points, compact indexed syntax, and richer network semantics as explicit deferred boundaries.
Accept: A fresh clone has a clear public entry point, settled license and repository metadata, a portable validation path, a canonical public example, green automated checks, an offline viewer, and no unexplained local-only files. The release gate reports data and unresolved decisions rather than silently filling gaps.
-> publish/handoff
@guidance text/markdown ./guidance/ml-project-workflow.md
@guidance text/markdown ./spec/grimoire.md
@guidance text/markdown ./grammar/grimoire.md
---
[publish/handoff] Human GitHub publication handoff (notstarted)
Files: GitHub repository settings and the local Git remote.
Do: After the release gate passes, create or confirm the public repository using the settled owner, name, remote URL, license, and version posture. Add the intended remote, push the reviewed branch, confirm the default branch and Actions workflow, and create a first release or tag only if the release posture calls for one. Do not place credentials, tokens, or private sibling paths in the repository.
Accept: The remote points to the intended public repository; the default branch renders the README and links; the first workflow run is green; the selected license is visible; and the public repository contains only the reviewed publication artifacts.
-> publish/verify
---
[publish/verify] Run the publication release gate (notstarted)
Files: The complete tracked repository and the acceptance commands recorded here.
Do: Run the focused checks after all publication edits: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace`, `cargo metadata --locked --no-deps`, and the workspace package check selected by the metadata task. Validate this VINE with the repository's VINE validator. Run `git diff --check`, inspect tracked files, and perform the direct offline viewer smoke check. Record command versions, exit status, and any remaining deferred or unresolved boundary.
Accept: Every command passes; the VINE validates; the package metadata and package contents are suitable for publication; the viewer opens without a build step, server, dependency, or network request; no generated or local-only artifact is tracked; and the final worktree contains only reviewed changes.
-> publish/scope
-> publish/metadata
-> publish/legal
-> publish/readme
-> publish/examples
-> publish/docs
-> publish/ci
-> publish/viewer
-> publish/hygiene
---
[publish/scope] Set public identity and release posture (notstarted)
Files: The publication decisions recorded in this VINE and the values consumed by release metadata.
Do: Confirm the GitHub owner, repository name, canonical remote URL, public project name, package version policy, pre-release wording, license choice, and Rust toolchain policy. Decide whether the tracked `.vscode/mcp.json` integration is part of the public product, an optional local development aid, or a file to remove. Record only settled values; leave no guessed GitHub URL, author identity, license holder, or stability claim in downstream artifacts.
Accept: Each publication identity and release-posture field has one explicit decision or an explicit human gate. Downstream tasks can consume the decisions without inventing repository ownership, license text, version semantics, or toolchain promises.
---
[publish/metadata] Align package and repository metadata (notstarted)
Files: Cargo.toml and crates/grimoire/Cargo.toml.
Do: Apply the settled public identity, description, license expression, repository or homepage metadata, package version, and declared Rust toolchain policy to the manifests. Keep workspace inheritance valid and avoid metadata that points outside the package or claims a stable language contract. Check the resulting package metadata and package file list with Cargo.
Accept: `cargo metadata --locked --no-deps` reports the intended package identity; the package manifest has no placeholder or private path; the license and version match the settled posture; and the selected `cargo package` command succeeds with the intended public files.
-> publish/scope
-> publish/legal
---
[publish/legal] Add the public license and attribution surface (notstarted)
Files: LICENSE and any narrowly required attribution file.
Do: Add the exact license text selected by the scope decision. Add attribution only where the repository's own authorship or included material requires it, using verified names and sources. Keep third-party paper citations in provenance and documentation rather than inventing ownership claims in the license file.
Accept: A fresh clone contains the complete selected license at the repository root; manifests and README references agree with it; attribution is sufficient for included material; and no copyright holder or third-party permission is guessed.
-> publish/scope
---
[publish/readme] Create the public repository entry point (notstarted)
Files: README.md.
Do: Write the shortest useful front door for a new reader: what Grimoire is, its pre-release/static status, the current Rust quickstart, how to open the offline viewer, how to follow the ML planning workflow, and links to the normative specification, grammar, proposals, reference fixtures, and license. State the non-runtime boundary plainly. Link to existing documents instead of duplicating their vocabulary or decisions.
Accept: A reader arriving at the GitHub root can understand the artifact, run the documented Rust checks, open the viewer locally, and navigate to the authoritative documents. Every relative link resolves, the README does not promise runtime execution or complete empirical measurement, and its status wording matches the scope decision.
-> publish/scope
-> publish/legal
---
[publish/examples] Expose one canonical public description (notstarted)
Files: examples/reference.grimoire and crates/grimoire/tests/reference_validation.rs.
Do: Extract the canonical reference description from the whole-system fixture into a public example using the reviewed grammar. Choose one source of truth for the example and test, or make the relationship mechanically obvious. Preserve the shared-address, layer, schema, cut, analysis-input, and deferred-boundary distinctions already exercised by the fixture. Do not add paper-specific core kinds or runtime behavior merely to make the example shorter.
Accept: The public example parses, validates against `prototype_schemas()`, evaluates its layers, round-trips through canonical serialization, and exercises a representative valid cut plus the visible non-cut failure. The test and example cannot silently diverge, and the example is understandable from the README and workflow guide.
-> publish/scope
---
[publish/docs] Audit public documentation and boundary claims (notstarted)
Files: spec/grimoire.md, grammar/grimoire.md, proposals/**/*.md, and guidance/*.md.
Do: Review links, document status labels, terminology, code snippets, and publication-facing claims across the existing documentation. Keep the core specification authoritative, keep the grammar and proposals visibly provisional where they are provisional, and make the static/offline/runtime/measurement boundaries agree with the implementation. Remove stale internal wording only when the repository evidence supports the change; do not settle an open design gap by prose alone.
Accept: All repository-relative links resolve; documentation identifies which artifact is normative, which is design guidance, and which is an implementation checkpoint; code examples use the current API or grammar; no document claims an unimplemented runtime or continuous analysis contract; and the ML workflow guide is discoverable from the public entry point.
-> publish/scope
---
[publish/ci] Add a reproducible GitHub Actions baseline (notstarted)
Files: .github/workflows/ci.yml.
Do: Add a small workflow for the declared Rust toolchain that runs formatting, strict Clippy, workspace tests, locked metadata, and the selected package check on pull requests and the default branch. Keep the workflow independent of the sibling `../scry` checkout, local model caches, network services, and the static viewer's optional browser inspection. Use action and toolchain versions consistent with the scope decision.
Accept: A fresh GitHub runner can execute the workflow from the repository alone; all required commands are explicit; failures are non-silent; the workflow has no secrets or developer-machine paths; and its commands match the local release gate.
-> publish/scope
-> publish/metadata
---
[publish/viewer] Verify the static viewer as a public artifact (notstarted)
Files: viz/index.html.
Do: Inspect the viewer directly from the workspace at desktop and narrow widths. Exercise corpus selection, primary and comparison views, address focus, group collapse, metadata and analysis toggles, and clear-focus behavior. Confirm the embedded model remains static and the page makes no network requests or dependency calls. Update only concrete issues found by this check, without turning the viewer into an editor or runtime.
Accept: `viz/index.html` opens directly with no build step, server, dependency, or network request; the V-JEPA, cross-paper, and opaque/deferred states remain inspectable; controls work at desktop and mobile widths; page-level overflow and text overlap are absent; and no new visualization-specific core kind or runtime semantics is introduced.
---
[publish/hygiene] Remove publication hazards and local coupling (notstarted)
Files: .gitignore and .vscode/mcp.json, plus the tracked-file inventory.
Do: Audit the tracked tree for secrets, generated output, local databases, editor-only state, sibling checkout assumptions, and machine-specific caches. Keep `target/` and `scry.redb` ignored. Resolve the public fate of `.vscode/mcp.json` according to the scope decision: make it portable, document it as optional local tooling, or remove it. Do not delete useful user configuration without recording the reason.
Accept: `git ls-files` contains no generated build output, local database, credential, private path, or unexplained machine-specific artifact; ignore rules cover the intended local outputs; any retained editor integration works or is explicitly optional from a fresh clone; and the repository can be checked without the sibling `../scry` tree.
-> publish/scope
---
